WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.914
A museum catalogue runs a fast image lookup in
isolation.

00:00:03.914 --> 00:00:10.300
During a school booking event, however, 600 visitors
arrive together and compete for the same

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:11.742
database connections.

00:00:11.742 --> 00:00:16.686
The lookup algorithm has not changed, but its
operating conditions have.

00:00:16.686 --> 00:00:23.279
A service must keep accepting work over time, share
finite resources among requests, and respond

00:00:23.279 --> 00:00:25.613
acceptably while arrivals overlap.

00:00:25.613 --> 00:00:31.104
A successful single-user demonstration therefore
does not establish service capacity.

00:00:31.104 --> 00:00:37.305
Begin the capacity discussion with a workload: which
operations arrive, how often, and with what

00:00:37.305 --> 00:00:39.501
acceptable latency and error rate?

00:00:39.501 --> 00:00:43.441
Suppose browsing costs much less than exporting a
collection.

00:00:43.441 --> 00:00:49.642
Two tests at 200 requests per second can produce
very different results if their operation mixes

00:00:49.642 --> 00:00:50.094
differ.

00:00:50.113 --> 00:00:55.797
Record both completed throughput and response-time
distributions, including slow requests.

00:00:55.797 --> 00:00:59.082
Then increase arrivals until waiting or errors rise.

00:00:59.082 --> 00:01:05.397
This experiment distinguishes an audience-size claim
from evidence that the architecture can support

00:01:05.397 --> 00:01:06.660
a particular demand.

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:11.925
The museum first considers a larger machine and then
a second application replica.

00:01:11.925 --> 00:01:17.833
If image processing consumes all available CPU, more
cores may help when the work can run in

00:01:17.833 --> 00:01:18.411
parallel.

00:01:18.411 --> 00:01:24.831
If every request waits on the same database lock,
another application machine can simply create more

00:01:24.831 --> 00:01:25.345
waiting.

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:31.764
Vertical and horizontal scaling are changes to
resource allocation; whether either helps depends on

00:01:31.764 --> 00:01:36.033
the resource or coordination point currently
limiting useful work.

00:01:36.033 --> 00:01:42.243
Consider a worker that spends 10 milliseconds
computing and 40 milliseconds waiting for a remote

00:01:42.243 --> 00:01:42.825
response.

00:01:42.825 --> 00:01:49.099
On one core, another request can use CPU during that
wait, so concurrency can improve utilization

00:01:49.099 --> 00:01:51.945
without making the first computation faster.

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:58.335
This is not a promise of a fivefold speedup: network
limits, scheduling and shared dependencies

00:01:58.335 --> 00:01:59.207
still matter.

00:01:59.207 --> 00:02:05.178
Before choosing larger machines or additional
replicas, measure CPU use, waiting time and

00:02:05.178 --> 00:02:05.916
contention.

00:02:05.916 --> 00:02:11.955
After changing capacity, repeat the same workload
test to see whether the bottleneck moved

00:02:11.955 --> 00:02:12.626
elsewhere.
